  "meta": {
    "title": "Monterey Canyon Redox Cline: Nitrite Oxidizers at the Twilight Edge",
    "date": "2026-07-30",
    "lesson": "In the Monterey Bay submarine canyon, a sharp oxycline sits where sinking organic matter has been stripped of oxygen by decomposition, but nitrite still accumulates faster than it's consumed. Nitrite-oxidizing bacteria (Nitrospina-like lineages) cluster in this narrow band, drawing chemical energy from nitrite oxidation while barely enough blue light filters down to matter. Above them, diatom-derived marine snow sinks slowly through a cold, dim water column; below, chemosynthesis takes over as the only real income. This level asks you to find and hold that narrow overlap zone - too shallow and there's no nitrite, too deep and the little light is gone - while grazers and phage still take their cut, and a founder must budget enzymes against a nitrogen-rich but lipid-poor particle field sinking from above.",
    "citation": "Beman & Francis (2006); Molari et al., water column chemoautotrophy studies, Monterey Bay MBARI time series",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Monterey Bay submarine canyon oxycline/nitricline microbial stratification, MBARI water column studies"
  },
